#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-ble-recut-arm — arm the BLE _ble_hard recut once its corpus precondition is MEASURED, not dated.
# reflex-cadence: 23 * * * *
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the board has no defer primitive.
# A [task] whose precondition is "48h from now" was posted to the board on 2026-08-24T22:38Z and
# re-dispatched to its owner 13 seconds later, because mesh-mind-control's scan has no not-before /
# snooze / after: token anywhere in it (grepped 2026-08-24: zero hits). A dated obligation left on the
# board is therefore not deferred, it is re-dispatched every dispatch cycle until its date arrives —
# ~48h of paid mind turns, each of which can only re-derive "too early" and post nothing. An
# obligation whose trigger is a CLOCK belongs to a scheduler, not to a board scan that has no clock in
# it; this tool is that scheduler, and the board task is closed until it fires.
#
# AND THE TRIGGER IS THE CORPUS, NOT THE CALENDAR. "2026-08-27T00:00Z" was itself an assumed band: it
# is 48h after the task was WRITTEN, but the RUN rows that form the residency denominator do not begin
# until mesh-land deploys the tape_ble_rssi run-row fix, which had not landed at that time (the live
# tape held 18 device rows and ZERO RUN rows). So the date could arrive with the window still empty.
# This tool gates on what the corpus actually holds:
#
#   span     = last RUN row - FIRST RUN row  (never the file's mtime, never the device rows)
#   cadence  = the RUN-row writer's OWN DECLARED cadence (mesh-room-sense's `# reflex-cadence:`
#              header), falling back to the smallest observed inter-row gap, and the reading names
#              which source it used
#   coverage = observed RUN rows / (span / cadence) — a span of 48h spanned by two rows is a dead
#              reflex, not a window; a bare span cannot tell those apart
#
# THE CADENCE MUST NOT BE THE MEDIAN OF THE GAPS, and this is the whole trap: a median (or any other
# central statistic) of the observed gaps ADAPTS TO THE HOLES it is supposed to expose. Two rows 49h
# apart have a median gap of 49h, so expected==observed and coverage reads a perfect 1.000; drop two
# of every three runs and the median simply becomes 15min and coverage reads 1.000 again. Both were
# measured going green against the first cut of this file. A coverage denominator has to come from
# OUTSIDE the sample it is judging — the writer's declaration, or at worst the tightest spacing the
# writer ever achieved. The min-gap fallback still has one honest blind spot, asserted in --test
# rather than papered over: it catches a tape that ran and went DARK, but UNIFORM thinning leaves no
# tight gap to compare against and re-reads as a slower writer. That is why every reading names its
# `cadence_src=`; a `measured-min` window is a weaker claim than a `declared` one.
#
# The tape is TAIL-TRIMMED (sliding window, ~5000 rows ≈ 4 days at the observed cadence), so once the
# real first row ages out, `first_run` becomes LATER than the true deploy instant and the span reads
# SHORTER. The gate therefore under-claims as the window slides — it can be late, never early.
#
# AND DURATION x DENSITY IS STILL NOT READINESS — THE CORPUS MUST CONTAIN A SUBJECT.
# The first cut of this file gated on span and coverage alone, and both are properties of the WRITER,
# not of the material. Measured 2026-08-27: the copy of this tool on phaedra — a VPS with no BLE
# adapter, whose every run therefore writes `RUN absent` and not one device row — read span_h=49.50,
# coverage=1.000, state=armed, and posted the recut obligation onto mesh-home's board. A perfectly
# dense window of NOTHING. The same blindness in its milder form is live on mesh-home: 1458 rows hold
# seven distinct names of which exactly ONE matches the live MESH_PERSON_RE, so the classifier this
# arms would be fitted to n=1 identity — a threshold that separates a single point from nothing is a
# constant wearing a claim's clothes, and no amount of further running fixes it.
# A classifier's readiness is a property of what its corpus CONTAINS, never of how long it ran, so
# two content terms gate alongside the window terms:
#
#   devices  = device rows in the window. Zero means BLIND, whatever the RUN rows say — and note the
#              run STATE is not the discriminator: `absent` is honestly "the scan saw nothing", which
#              in an empty room is a real reading and on an adapterless host is total blindness. The
#              fact that separates them is whether ANY advertisement has EVER landed.
#   subjects = DISTINCT NAMES matching the live MESH_PERSON_RE — the gate's actual domain. Name-keyed,
#              never MAC-keyed: the one subject in the mesh-home corpus rotates its address across 32
#              MACs in 49h, so a per-MAC count reads 32 identities where there is one.
#
# The pattern is READ FROM THE LIVE LIBRARY (mesh-patterns.sh), never typed here — mesh-room-sense's
# own in-file default matches nothing in the tape and the sourced library's value is what actually
# gates. If the library cannot be read the subject count is UNKNOWN and the tool refuses to arm: an
# unreadable domain must fail LOUD, never fall through to a post.
#
# State (published in every reading, and rc):
#   undeployed   0 RUN rows — the denominator fix is not live yet. UNKNOWN coverage, never "no runs".
#   accumulating span < need
#   sparse       span ok, coverage below floor — the window exists but the writer was mostly dark
#   blind        window ok, ZERO device rows ever — a dense window of nothing is not a window
#   no-pattern   the person pattern could not be read, so the corpus's domain is UNKNOWN
#   one-class    window ok, fewer than the floor of distinct SUBJECT identities — nothing to falsify
#                a threshold against. Not fixable by waiting; only a new identity clears it.
#   armed        gate met, [task] posted this run
#   posted       gate met, already posted (marker present) — idempotent, never a second obligation
set -uo pipefail

# THIS TOOL IS LAUNCHED BY CRON AND CRON'S PATH DOES NOT CARRY ~/.local/bin. The cron line names the
# tool ABSOLUTELY, so the launch always worked and only the INNER calls broke: the one-shot announce
# died `line 141: mesh-chat: command not found` at the very tick the 48h gate opened
# (2026-08-26T23:23:01Z), and because the post is `mesh-chat ... && : > MARK` the marker was never
# written, so the obligation re-attempted hourly and could never reach the board. Two more callees sit
# on the same PATH: `mesh-trace` below (whose failure was INVISIBLE, wrapped `2>/dev/null || true`)
# and the `command -v mesh-room-sense` that sources the DECLARED cadence — under cron that lookup
# fails too and every reading silently demoted itself to the weaker `cadence_src=measured-min`.
# One prepend covers all three and any callee added later. It is $HOME-derived, never $0-derived, so a
# subject driven under a remapped HOME resolves the tools the harness put there — which is what the
# cron-PATH arm in --test depends on.
MESH_BIN="${MESH_BIN:-$HOME/.local/bin}"
case ":$PATH:" in
  *":$MESH_BIN:"*) ;;
  *) PATH="$MESH_BIN:$PATH"; export PATH ;;
esac

TAPE="${MESH_ROOM_BLE_TAPE:-$HOME/.mesh/room-sense-ble-rssi-tape.log}"
MARK="${MESH_BLE_RECUT_MARK:-$HOME/.mesh/.ble-recut-armed}"
NEED_H="${MESH_BLE_RECUT_NEED_H:-48}"
# Coverage floor: NOT a threshold of the classifier this arms (those must come from the live corpus
# via mesh-series-stats --claims, per the task). This is a floor on the WINDOW's own honesty — how
# much of the span the writer was actually awake for — and it is published in the reading either way.
COV_FLOOR="${MESH_BLE_RECUT_COV_FLOOR:-0.5}"
# Where the DECLARED cadence is read from — the writer's own header, never a number typed here.
CAD_SRC_FILE="${MESH_BLE_RECUT_CADENCE_FILE:-$(command -v mesh-room-sense 2>/dev/null || echo "$HOME/lte-workstation/scripts/mesh-room-sense")}"
# Subject floor: the number of DISTINCT person-class identities the corpus must hold before a
# two-axis classifier may be cut against it. Below 2 there is nothing for a threshold to separate, so
# any value fitted to it is unfalsifiable by construction. This is a floor on the corpus's
# COMPOSITION, not a threshold of the classifier — those still come from mesh-series-stats --claims.
SUBJ_FLOOR="${MESH_BLE_RECUT_SUBJ_FLOOR:-2}"
# The person pattern comes from the LIVE library, on the same ladder mesh-room-sense itself uses.
PATTERNS_FILE="${MESH_BLE_RECUT_PATTERNS_FILE:-$HOME/.local/bin/mesh-patterns.sh}"

# person_re — echo the live MESH_PERSON_RE, or return 1. NEVER a literal fallback: a default typed
# here would silently redefine the gate's domain, and mesh-room-sense's own in-file default is
# already measured to match nothing in this tape.
person_re() {
  local re=""
  if [ -r "$PATTERNS_FILE" ]; then
    # Sourced in a SUBSHELL: mesh-patterns.sh exports a large set of names and this tool must not
    # inherit them into its own scope.
    re="$( . "$PATTERNS_FILE" >/dev/null 2>&1; printf '%s' "${MESH_PERSON_RE:-}" )"
  fi
  [ -n "$re" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$re"
}

# NO BACKTICKS IN THIS STRING: mesh-chat command-substitutes them (memory
# mesh-chat-executes-backticks-in-the-message), so a backticked identifier here would be RUN, not
# posted, and the board would carry its output instead of the obligation.
TASK_TEXT='[task] room-sense-ble-hard-on-movement-recut: the BLE residency corpus now holds the window (see fields below). Replace _ble_hard'"'"'s NAME test in mesh-room-sense (the ble_nameless_only leg) with a MOVEMENT/persistence test per MAC: RSSI variance + continuous-residency, residency = seen-runs over RUN rows in the window, degraded RUN rows EXCLUDED from the denominator rather than counted as absence. A device resident and flat for days is furniture whatever it is called. Derive BOTH thresholds from the live corpus with mesh-series-stats --claims and bind the CLAIM, never a quoted constant. owner: mesh-land/senses'

# declared_cadence_min — the RUN-row writer's own `# reflex-cadence:` minute field, as minutes.
# Empty when it cannot be read; the caller then falls back and SAYS so.
declared_cadence_min() {
  local line f n
  [ -r "$CAD_SRC_FILE" ] || return 1
  line="$(grep -m1 -E '^#[[:space:]]*reflex-cadence:' "$CAD_SRC_FILE" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  f="$(printf '%s' "$line" | sed -E 's/^#[[:space:]]*reflex-cadence:[[:space:]]*//' | awk '{print $1}')"
  [ -n "$f" ] || return 1
  case "$f" in
    */*) printf '%s' "${f##*/}"; return 0 ;;                 # 2-59/5 -> 5
    '*') printf '1'; return 0 ;;
    *)   n="$(printf '%s' "$f" | tr ',' '\n' | grep -c '')"  # 8,28,48 -> 3 fires/hour -> 20min
         [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ] || return 1
         awk -v n="$n" 'BEGIN{printf "%.2f", 60/n}'; return 0 ;;
  esac
}

# read_tape → sets RUN_N SPAN_H CADENCE_M CAD_SRC COV FIRST LAST; rc 2 when no RUN rows exist.
read_tape() {
  RUN_N=0; SPAN_H="na"; CADENCE_M="na"; CAD_SRC="na"; COV="na"; FIRST="na"; LAST="na"
  DEV_N=0; IDENT_N=0; SUBJ_N="na"; SUBJ_SRC="na"; OBS_F="na"
  [ -r "$TAPE" ] || return 2
  local eps
  eps="$(grep -P '\tRUN\t' "$TAPE" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1 \
        | while read -r t; do date -u -d "$t" +%s 2>/dev/null; done)"
  [ -n "$eps" ] || return 2
  RUN_N="$(printf '%s\n' "$eps" | grep -c '')"
  FIRST="$(printf '%s\n' "$eps" | head -1)"
  LAST="$(printf '%s\n' "$eps" | tail -1)"
  local span=$(( LAST - FIRST ))
  FIRST="$(date -u -d "@$FIRST" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
  LAST="$(date -u -d "@$LAST" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
  SPAN_H="$(awk -v s="$span" 'BEGIN{printf "%.2f", s/3600}')"
  if CADENCE_M="$(declared_cadence_min)" && [ -n "$CADENCE_M" ]; then
    CAD_SRC="declared"
  elif [ "$RUN_N" -ge 2 ]; then
    # Fallback: the TIGHTEST spacing the writer ever achieved. Not central — a minimum cannot be
    # moved by adding holes, so a sparse window still reads sparse.
    CADENCE_M="$(printf '%s\n' "$eps" | awk 'NR>1{d=$1-p; if(m==""||d<m)m=d} {p=$1} END{if(m==""){print "na"}else{printf "%.2f", m/60}}')"
    CAD_SRC="measured-min"
  else
    CADENCE_M="na"; CAD_SRC="unknown"
  fi
  # --- CONTENT terms: what the window actually HOLDS -------------------------------------------
  # `ok` runs over all runs. Published beside the value, never folded into coverage: a run that
  # executed and saw nothing is a real observation of an empty room on one node and total blindness
  # on an adapterless one, and the RUN state alone cannot separate those two.
  OBS_F="$(awk -F'\t' '$2=="RUN"{t++; if($3=="ok")o++} END{ if(t>0) printf "%.3f", o/t; else print "na" }' "$TAPE" 2>/dev/null)"
  DEV_N="$(awk -F'\t' '$2!="RUN"' "$TAPE" 2>/dev/null | grep -c '')"
  # NAME-keyed, never MAC-keyed: the one subject in this corpus rotates across 32 MACs in 49h, so a
  # per-MAC count reports 32 identities where there is one (memory
  # a-rotating-identity-fragments-a-per-key-residency).
  IDENT_N="$(awk -F'\t' '$2!="RUN"{print $3}' "$TAPE" 2>/dev/null | sort -u | grep -c '')"
  local _re
  if _re="$(person_re)"; then
    SUBJ_SRC="library"
    SUBJ_N="$(awk -F'\t' '$2!="RUN"{print $3}' "$TAPE" 2>/dev/null | sort -u \
              | grep -Ec "$_re" || true)"
    [ -n "$SUBJ_N" ] || SUBJ_N=0
  else
    SUBJ_SRC="unknown"; SUBJ_N="na"
  fi

  [ "$CADENCE_M" != "na" ] || return 0
  COV="$(awk -v n="$RUN_N" -v s="$span" -v c="$CADENCE_M" \
    'BEGIN{ if(c+0<=0||s<=0){print "na";exit} e=s/(c*60)+1; printf "%.3f", (e>0)? n/e : 0 }')"
  return 0
}

verdict() {
  local rc
  read_tape; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" = 2 ]; then STATE="undeployed"; return 0; fi
  if awk -v a="$SPAN_H" -v b="$NEED_H" 'BEGIN{exit !(a+0 < b+0)}'; then STATE="accumulating"; return 0; fi
  if [ "$COV" = "na" ] || awk -v a="$COV" -v b="$COV_FLOOR" 'BEGIN{exit !(a+0 < b+0)}'; then
    STATE="sparse"; return 0; fi
  # CONTENT gates run AFTER the window gates, and that order is load-bearing: a corpus of two rows
  # cannot credibly tell you it holds no subjects, so "sparse" must be said before "blind".
  if [ "${DEV_N:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then STATE="blind"; return 0; fi
  if [ "${SUBJ_SRC:-unknown}" != "library" ]; then STATE="no-pattern"; return 0; fi
  if [ "${SUBJ_N:-0}" -lt "$SUBJ_FLOOR" ]; then STATE="one-class"; return 0; fi
  if [ -e "$MARK" ]; then STATE="posted"; else STATE="armed"; fi
  return 0
}

render() {
  printf '%s ble-recut-arm state=%s run_rows=%s first_run=%s last_run=%s span_h=%s need_h=%s cadence_min=%s cadence_src=%s coverage=%s cov_floor=%s obs_frac=%s device_rows=%s identities=%s subjects=%s subj_floor=%s subj_src=%s tape=%s\n' \
    "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$STATE" "$RUN_N" "$FIRST" "$LAST" "$SPAN_H" "$NEED_H" "$CADENCE_M" "$CAD_SRC" "$COV" "$COV_FLOOR" \
    "${OBS_F:-na}" "${DEV_N:-na}" "${IDENT_N:-na}" "${SUBJ_N:-na}" "$SUBJ_FLOOR" "${SUBJ_SRC:-na}" "$TAPE"
}

case "${1:-}" in
  --help|-h) sed -n '2,40p' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
  --status)  verdict; render; exit 0 ;;
  --test)    ;;
  "")        ;;
  *) echo "mesh-ble-recut-arm: unknown option '$1'" >&2; exit 64 ;;
esac

if [ "${1:-}" != "--test" ]; then
  verdict
  render
  if [ "$STATE" = "armed" ]; then
    mesh-chat "$TASK_TEXT [corpus: run_rows=$RUN_N span_h=$SPAN_H cadence_min=$CADENCE_M/$CAD_SRC coverage=$COV obs_frac=$OBS_F device_rows=$DEV_N identities=$IDENT_N subjects=$SUBJ_N/$SUBJ_SRC first_run=$FIRST]" \
      && : > "$MARK"
    # `|| true` so a trace failure cannot cost the run (the marker is already written), but NOT
    # `2>/dev/null`: this call was equally bare and its death was silent for exactly that reason.
    mesh-trace "ble-recut armed: span_h=$SPAN_H coverage=$COV run_rows=$RUN_N — recut task posted" || true
  fi
  exit 0
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- --test
_f=0
_t="$(mktemp -d)"; trap 'rm -rf "$_t"' EXIT
# _mk builds a corpus that carries TWO distinct subject identities, because a window with fewer is
# no longer a window (see the composition gate). The names are person-class under the live
# MESH_PERSON_RE as well as under the fixture below, so the in-process arms and the end-to-end
# cron-PATH arm (which reads the real library) agree.
_mk() { # _mk <file> <n_rows> <start_epoch> <step_sec>
  local f="$1" n="$2" s="$3" st="$4" i ts
  : > "$f"
  for ((i=0;i<n;i++)); do
    ts="$(date -u -d "@$((s+i*st))" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    printf '%s\tRUN\tok\t3/2\n' "$ts" >> "$f"
    printf '%s\tAA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF\tPixel 7\t-60\n' "$ts" >> "$f"
    printf '%s\tBB:CC:DD:EE:FF:AA\tGalaxy Buds Pro\t-71\n' "$ts" >> "$f"
  done
}
# _mk_runs — RUN rows ONLY: the adapterless host. This is the phaedra corpus, verbatim in shape.
_mk_runs() { # _mk_runs <file> <n_rows> <start_epoch> <step_sec> [run_state]
  local f="$1" n="$2" s="$3" st="$4" rs="${5:-absent}" i
  : > "$f"
  for ((i=0;i<n;i++)); do
    printf '%s\tRUN\t%s\t0/0\n' "$(date -u -d "@$((s+i*st))" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$rs" >> "$f"
  done
}
_now=$(date -u +%s)
# A stand-in for the RUN-row writer's header, so the declared-cadence leg does not depend on
# whichever mesh-room-sense happens to be on this node's PATH.
printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# reflex-cadence: 2-59/5 * * * *\n' > "$_t/writer5"
printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# reflex-cadence: 8,28,48 * * * *\n' > "$_t/writer20"
CAD_SRC_FILE="$_t/writer5"
# The person pattern under test is a FIXTURE, not the live library: an arm that reads the live
# MESH_PERSON_RE would go red the day someone edits mesh-patterns.sh, for reasons that have nothing
# to do with this gate. The end-to-end arm 11 still drives the real library.
printf 'MESH_PERSON_RE=%s\n' "'Pixel|Galaxy Buds'" > "$_t/patterns.sh"
PATTERNS_FILE="$_t/patterns.sh"

# arm 1 — no RUN rows at all (device rows only) is UNDEPLOYED, never "the runs did not happen"
TAPE="$_t/a.log"; MARK="$_t/a.mark"
printf '2026-08-24T22:05:13Z\tAA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF\tThing\t-60\n' > "$TAPE"
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "undeployed" ] && [ "$RUN_N" = 0 ] && [ "$COV" = "na" ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL undeployed: state=$STATE run_rows=$RUN_N coverage=$COV"; _f=1; }
[ -e "$MARK" ] && { echo "  FAIL undeployed: marker written"; _f=1; }

# arm 2 — a short window is ACCUMULATING, and it must not post
TAPE="$_t/b.log"; MARK="$_t/b.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 12 $((_now-3600)) 300
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "accumulating" ] || { echo "  FAIL accumulating: state=$STATE span_h=$SPAN_H"; _f=1; }

# arm 3 — a 49h SPAN spanned by TWO rows is a dead reflex, not a window: SPARSE, and it must not
#         post. This is the leg a span-only gate cannot go red on, and the leg a MEDIAN-derived
#         cadence cannot go red on either (median gap == the whole span => coverage 1.000).
TAPE="$_t/c.log"; MARK="$_t/c.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 2 $((_now-50*3600)) $((49*3600))
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "sparse" ] || { echo "  FAIL sparse: state=$STATE span_h=$SPAN_H coverage=$COV"; _f=1; }
awk -v a="$SPAN_H" 'BEGIN{exit !(a+0 >= 48)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL sparse: span should exceed need, got $SPAN_H"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$COV" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 < 0.01)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL sparse: two rows over 49h must read near-zero coverage, got $COV"; _f=1; }

# arm 3b — THE FOUNDING CASE: a PERFECTLY DENSE 50h window of RUN rows with ZERO device rows. This
#          is phaedra verbatim — a VPS with no BLE adapter, 595/595 runs `absent` — which the
#          span+coverage gate read as coverage=1.000/armed and posted the recut obligation off.
#          Coverage is a property of the writer; it cannot see that the material is empty.
TAPE="$_t/blind.log"; MARK="$_t/blind.mark"
_mk_runs "$TAPE" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300 absent
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "blind" ] || { echo "  FAIL blind: a dense window of nothing must not arm, got state=$STATE coverage=$COV"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$COV" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 0.9)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL blind: the fixture must be DENSE, else it is the sparse arm wearing a new name (cov=$COV)"; _f=1; }
[ "$DEV_N" = 0 ] && [ "$IDENT_N" = 0 ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL blind: device_rows=$DEV_N identities=$IDENT_N"; _f=1; }
[ "$OBS_F" = "0.000" ] || { echo "  FAIL blind: obs_frac should be 0 on an all-absent tape, got $OBS_F"; _f=1; }
[ -e "$MARK" ] && { echo "  FAIL blind: marker written"; _f=1; }
# ...and the run STATE is not the discriminator: relabel every run `ok` and it is STILL blind,
# because the fact that separates an empty room from a dead adapter is whether an advertisement
# ever landed, never the word the writer chose.
_mk_runs "$TAPE" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300 ok
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "blind" ] || { echo "  FAIL blind: state must not rest on the run-state word, got $STATE (obs_frac=$OBS_F)"; _f=1; }
[ "$OBS_F" = "1.000" ] || { echo "  FAIL blind: obs_frac should be 1 on an all-ok tape, got $OBS_F"; _f=1; }

# arm 3c — THE LIVE mesh-home CASE: a dense window that holds devices, but only ONE identity in the
#          gate's domain (the others are furniture, matching no person pattern). A two-axis
#          classifier fitted to a single point is a constant wearing a claim's clothes.
TAPE="$_t/one.log"; MARK="$_t/one.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300
sed -i 's/\tGalaxy Buds Pro\t/\t[TV] Samsung 5 Series (40)\t/' "$TAPE"
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "one-class" ] || { echo "  FAIL one-class: state=$STATE subjects=$SUBJ_N identities=$IDENT_N"; _f=1; }
[ "$IDENT_N" = 2 ] && [ "$SUBJ_N" = 1 ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL one-class: 2 identities of which 1 is a subject, got identities=$IDENT_N subjects=$SUBJ_N"; _f=1; }
[ -e "$MARK" ] && { echo "  FAIL one-class: marker written"; _f=1; }

# arm 3d — an identity is NAME-keyed, never MAC-keyed. The single live subject rotates its address
#          across 32 MACs in 49h, so a per-MAC count would report 32 subjects and clear a floor of 2
#          off ONE device — the fragmentation that would have re-armed this gate on the same corpus.
TAPE="$_t/rot.log"; MARK="$_t/rot.mark"
: > "$TAPE"
for ((_i=0;_i<600;_i++)); do
  _ts="$(date -u -d "@$((_now-50*3600+_i*300))" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
  printf '%s\tRUN\tok\t1/1\n' "$_ts" >> "$TAPE"
  printf '%s\t%02X:11:22:33:44:55\tPixel 7\t-60\n' "$_ts" "$((_i % 32))" >> "$TAPE"
done
verdict
[ "$SUBJ_N" = 1 ] && [ "$IDENT_N" = 1 ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL name-keyed: 32 MACs of ONE name must count as one identity, got identities=$IDENT_N subjects=$SUBJ_N"; _f=1; }
[ "$STATE" = "one-class" ] || { echo "  FAIL name-keyed: state=$STATE"; _f=1; }

# arm 3e — the DOMAIN is read from the live library and an unreadable library is UNKNOWN, never a
#          fall-through to a post. An in-file default here would silently redefine what the gate
#          gates on (mesh-room-sense's own in-file default is measured to match nothing in this tape).
PATTERNS_FILE="$_t/no-such-patterns.sh"
TAPE="$_t/d.log"; MARK="$_t/np.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "no-pattern" ] || { echo "  FAIL no-pattern: unreadable domain must not arm, got state=$STATE"; _f=1; }
[ "$SUBJ_N" = "na" ] && [ "$SUBJ_SRC" = "unknown" ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL no-pattern: subjects=$SUBJ_N src=$SUBJ_SRC — an unknown domain must render na"; _f=1; }
[ -e "$MARK" ] && { echo "  FAIL no-pattern: marker written"; _f=1; }
PATTERNS_FILE="$_t/patterns.sh"

# arm 4 — a full, dense 50h window ARMS, cadence taken from the writer's DECLARED header
TAPE="$_t/d.log"; MARK="$_t/d.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "armed" ] || { echo "  FAIL armed: state=$STATE span_h=$SPAN_H coverage=$COV"; _f=1; }
[ "$CAD_SRC" = "declared" ] || { echo "  FAIL armed: cadence source should be declared, got $CAD_SRC"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$CADENCE_M" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 4.9 && c+0 < 5.1)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL armed: declared cadence should be 5min, got $CADENCE_M"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$COV" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 0.9)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL armed: dense window coverage should be ~1, got $COV"; _f=1; }
[ "$SUBJ_N" = 2 ] && [ "$SUBJ_SRC" = "library" ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL armed: two subject identities expected, got subjects=$SUBJ_N src=$SUBJ_SRC"; _f=1; }

# arm 5 — HALF-DARK: two of every three runs missing at the same span reads SPARSE. A median-derived
#         cadence would have re-fitted itself to 15min here and called it a perfect window.
TAPE="$_t/e.log"; MARK="$_t/e.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300
# Thin by RUN, never by LINE: _mk writes three lines per run (the run row plus two subject rows),
# so a line-modulus silently drops the device rows out of the surviving runs too and the arm ends up
# measuring the composition gate instead of the cadence one.
awk '(NR-1)%9<3' "$TAPE" > "$TAPE.k" && mv "$TAPE.k" "$TAPE"
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "sparse" ] || { echo "  FAIL dark-window: state=$STATE coverage=$COV"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$COV" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 0.28 && c+0 < 0.40)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL dark-window: 1-in-3 coverage should read ~0.33, got $COV"; _f=1; }

# arm 6 — the declared cadence is PARSED, not assumed: a list-form header (3 fires/hour) yields 20min,
#         so a 20-min-cadence writer is not accused of a 4x hole
CAD_SRC_FILE="$_t/writer20"
TAPE="$_t/f.log"; MARK="$_t/f.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 150 $((_now-50*3600)) 1200
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "armed" ] || { echo "  FAIL list-cadence: state=$STATE cadence=$CADENCE_M coverage=$COV"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$CADENCE_M" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 19.9 && c+0 < 20.1)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL list-cadence: expected 20min, got $CADENCE_M"; _f=1; }

# arm 7 — no declared header reachable → the fallback is the TIGHTEST observed gap, named as such.
#         It catches a tape that RAN and then went dark (min gap survives the hole)...
CAD_SRC_FILE="$_t/nofile"
TAPE="$_t/g.log"; MARK="$_t/g.mark"
_mk "$TAPE" 200 $((_now-50*3600)) 300          # ~16.6h dense, then nothing until the closing row
printf '%s\tRUN\tok\t2/1\n' "$(date -u -d "@$_now" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" >> "$TAPE"
verdict
[ "$CAD_SRC" = "measured-min" ] || { echo "  FAIL fallback: cadence source=$CAD_SRC"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$CADENCE_M" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 4.9 && c+0 < 5.1)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL fallback: tightest gap should be 5min, got $CADENCE_M"; _f=1; }
[ "$STATE" = "sparse" ] || { echo "  FAIL fallback: ran-then-went-dark should be sparse, got $STATE ($COV)"; _f=1; }

# arm 7b — ...and it is BLIND to UNIFORM thinning, which is the honest limit of a min-gap denominator:
#          decimate every tick evenly and there is no tight gap left to compare against, so the same
#          half-dark tape that the DECLARED cadence called sparse (arm 5) arms here. Asserted, not
#          hidden — this is exactly why `cadence_src=` is published in every reading.
TAPE="$_t/e.log"; MARK="$_t/e2.mark"
verdict
[ "$CAD_SRC" = "measured-min" ] && [ "$STATE" = "armed" ] \
  || { echo "  FAIL fallback-blindspot: expected measured-min/armed, got $CAD_SRC/$STATE"; _f=1; }
awk -v c="$CADENCE_M" 'BEGIN{exit !(c+0 > 14.9 && c+0 < 15.1)}' \
  || { echo "  FAIL fallback-blindspot: uniform thinning should re-read as a 15min cadence, got $CADENCE_M"; _f=1; }
CAD_SRC_FILE="$_t/writer5"

# arm 8 — IDEMPOTENCE: the marker turns armed into posted, so a second cron tick mints no second task
TAPE="$_t/d.log"; MARK="$_t/d.mark"; : > "$MARK"
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "posted" ] || { echo "  FAIL idempotent: state=$STATE"; _f=1; }

# arm 9 — an unreadable tape is UNDEPLOYED/UNKNOWN, never an armed post off a missing corpus
TAPE="$_t/nope.log"; MARK="$_t/g.mark"
verdict
[ "$STATE" = "undeployed" ] || { echo "  FAIL missing-tape: state=$STATE"; _f=1; }

# arm 10 — the reading always publishes its coverage terms AND names its cadence source
TAPE="$_t/b.log"; MARK="$_t/b.mark"; verdict
render | grep -q 'run_rows=.*span_h=.*cadence_min=.*cadence_src=.*coverage=.*cov_floor=' \
  || { echo "  FAIL render: coverage terms missing from the reading"; _f=1; }
render | grep -q 'obs_frac=.*device_rows=.*identities=.*subjects=.*subj_floor=.*subj_src=' \
  || { echo "  FAIL render: composition terms missing from the reading"; _f=1; }

# arm 11 — THE CRON-PATH ARM. Every arm above calls verdict() in-process, so not one of them ever
#          entered the announce block — which is where the tool actually broke. Cron carries no
#          ~/.local/bin, the tool is launched ABSOLUTELY (so it starts fine) and the callees inside it
#          were bare, so `mesh-chat` died `command not found` at the exact tick the gate opened and
#          `&& : > MARK` then withheld the marker forever. A --test that inherits the MIND'S PATH
#          cannot see any of this: the tools are already resolvable, so the bug is invisible.
#          Therefore drive the REAL armed edge end-to-end under `env -i` with cron's PATH, and put the
#          real tools in the FAKE HOME's .local/bin — not merely somewhere on PATH. That placement is
#          load-bearing: the subject EXPORTS its own PATH from $HOME, so a stub parked on the inherited
#          PATH would be shadowed the moment the prepend runs and the arm would be testing nothing.
_ch="$(command -v mesh-chat 2>/dev/null)"; _tr="$(command -v mesh-trace 2>/dev/null)"
_rs="$(command -v mesh-room-sense 2>/dev/null)"
if [ -z "$_ch" ] || [ -z "$_tr" ] || [ -z "$_rs" ]; then
  # NOT a skip. The arm's whole subject is tool resolution; declaring it green because the tools are
  # missing would be the same silence it exists to catch.
  echo "  FAIL cron-path: cannot stage the arm — mesh-chat/mesh-trace/mesh-room-sense not resolvable here"; _f=1
else
  # $0 resolved to an absolute path: under env -i with cron's PATH a bare $0 is not findable, and a
  # bare invocation resolves through the mind's PATH to the DEPLOYED twin rather than this file.
  _self="$0"; case "$_self" in */*) ;; *) _self="$(command -v -- "$_self")" ;; esac
  _self="$(readlink -f -- "$_self")"
  _fh="$_t/fakehome"; mkdir -p "$_fh/.local/bin" "$_fh/.mesh"
  ln -sf "$_ch" "$_fh/.local/bin/mesh-chat"
  ln -sf "$_tr" "$_fh/.local/bin/mesh-trace"
  ln -sf "$_rs" "$_fh/.local/bin/mesh-room-sense"
  # The composition gate reads the person pattern from $HOME/.local/bin/mesh-patterns.sh, so the fake
  # home must carry the REAL library — this arm is the one that drives the live domain end to end.
  _pt="$(command -v mesh-patterns.sh 2>/dev/null || echo "$HOME/.local/bin/mesh-patterns.sh")"
  [ -r "$_pt" ] && ln -sf "$_pt" "$_fh/.local/bin/mesh-patterns.sh" \
    || { echo "  FAIL cron-path: mesh-patterns.sh not readable — the gate's domain cannot be staged"; _f=1; }
  _mk "$_fh/.mesh/room-sense-ble-rssi-tape.log" 600 $((_now-50*3600)) 300
  _out="$(env -i HOME="$_fh" PATH=/usr/bin:/bin "$_self" 2>&1)"; _rc=$?
  _cl="$_fh/.mesh/chat.log"

  [ "$_rc" = 0 ] || { echo "  FAIL cron-path: rc=$_rc"; _f=1; }
  case "$_out" in *"command not found"*)
    echo "  FAIL cron-path: a callee is still bare under cron PATH -> $_out"; _f=1 ;;
  esac
  # THE TWO ASSERTIONS THE BUG WOULD HAVE FAILED: the post ARRIVED, and the marker EXISTS.
  [ -s "$_cl" ] && grep -q 'room-sense-ble-hard-on-movement-recut' "$_cl" \
    || { echo "  FAIL cron-path: the recut [task] never reached the board under cron PATH"; _f=1; }
  [ -e "$_fh/.mesh/.ble-recut-armed" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL cron-path: marker absent, so the obligation re-attempts every tick forever"; _f=1; }
  # The SILENT victim: mesh-trace was equally bare and wrapped 2>/dev/null || true.
  grep -q 'ble-recut armed' "$_fh/.mesh/traces.log" 2>/dev/null \
    || { echo "  FAIL cron-path: mesh-trace wrote nothing — its failure is the invisible one"; _f=1; }
  # The THIRD victim, and the one no error message ever named: `command -v mesh-room-sense` also fails
  # under cron PATH, so every cron reading silently demoted itself to the weaker measured-min cadence.
  case "$_out" in *"cadence_src=declared"*) ;;
    *) echo "  FAIL cron-path: cadence source should be declared under cron PATH, got -> $_out"; _f=1 ;;
  esac
  # The FOURTH thing cron's PATH can silently break: the pattern library is $HOME-derived too, and a
  # gate that cannot read its domain must say so rather than arm.
  case "$_out" in *"subj_src=library"*) ;;
    *) echo "  FAIL cron-path: the person pattern must resolve under cron PATH, got -> $_out"; _f=1 ;;
  esac
  # ...and a SECOND tick over the same fake home mints no second obligation.
  _n1="$(grep -c '' "$_cl" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  _out2="$(env -i HOME="$_fh" PATH=/usr/bin:/bin "$_self" 2>&1)"
  _n2="$(grep -c '' "$_cl" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  case "$_out2" in *"state=posted"*) ;;
    *) echo "  FAIL cron-path: second tick should read posted, got -> $_out2"; _f=1 ;;
  esac
  [ "$_n1" = "$_n2" ] || { echo "  FAIL cron-path: second tick posted again ($_n1 -> $_n2)"; _f=1; }
fi

[ "$_f" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAILED"; exit 1; }
echo "smoke-test: ok (16 arms: undeployed-vs-no-runs + accumulating + two-rows-over-49h-sparse + dense-window-of-NOTHING-is-blind-not-armed + run-state-word-is-not-the-discriminator + one-subject-identity-is-one-class + identity-is-NAME-keyed-not-MAC-keyed + unreadable-person-pattern-is-no-pattern-never-a-post + armed-dense-declared-cadence + half-dark-1-in-3-sparse + list-form-cadence-parsed-20min + no-header-falls-back-to-tightest-gap-catches-a-hole + fallback-is-blind-to-uniform-thinning + idempotent-marker + missing-tape-unknown + coverage-terms-and-cadence-source-always-published + armed-edge-driven-end-to-end-under-cron-PATH-post-arrives-and-marker-written)"
exit 0
